WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.071 --> 00:00:08.775
<v Daniel>You know, when you read a science fiction novel about cloning, it usually involves like some glowing vat of DNA in a sterile underground lab.</v>

2
00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:10.655
<v Maya>Oh, yeah, totally. The classic</v>

3
00:00:10.715 --> 00:00:24.012
<v Daniel>truth. And the goal is almost always to perfectly replicate a human body. But right now, like. Out here in the real world, startups and social scientists are attempting something arguably much more ambitious.</v>

4
00:00:24.213 --> 00:00:26.995
<v Maya>Yeah, they aren't trying to clone your body. They are trying to clone your mind.</v>

5
00:00:27.135 --> 00:00:29.377
<v Daniel>Exactly. They're building what are called digital twins.</v>

6
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:29.877
<v Maya>Right.</v>

7
00:00:29.997 --> 00:00:39.104
<v Daniel>So these are AI models, specifically large language models, that get fed a massive, highly detailed dossier about a real living person.</v>

8
00:00:39.565 --> 00:00:39.745
<v Maya>Mm-hmm.</v>

9
00:00:40.165 --> 00:00:50.851
<v Daniel>And the promise is that these A.I. twins will simulate exactly how you yes, you listen to this right now, how you would think, how you would vote, what moral tradeoffs you'd make and even what you would buy.</v>

10
00:00:51.291 --> 00:00:55.494
<v Maya>It is an incredibly seductive pitch. I mean, especially for anyone in research or marketing.</v>

11
00:00:55.534 --> 00:00:56.034
<v Daniel>Oh, for sure.</v>

12
00:00:56.274 --> 00:01:07.080
<v Maya>The core idea is that we can essentially replace expensive, messy, time consuming human research subjects with cheap, instantly available simulated ones.</v>

13
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.542
<v Daniel>Just generate them on demand.</v>

14
00:01:08.682 --> 00:01:23.420
<v Maya>Exactly. Say you want to know how a specific demographic in the Midwest will react to a new public policy or a new consumer product. Right. Instead of spending months running focus groups, you just spin up thousands of their digital</v>

15
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:24.522
<v Maya>twins and ask them.</v>

16
00:01:24.702 --> 00:01:29.865
<v Daniel>But today we're taking a deep dive into our source material to find out if this is actually working.</v>

17
00:01:30.025 --> 00:01:30.265
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

18
00:01:30.345 --> 00:01:39.309
<v Daniel>We have a stack of research in front of us. But our absolute north star for this conversation is a massive brand new paper.</v>

19
00:01:39.369 --> 00:01:40.030
